Test is now using PexClassAttribute to generate a code coverage report. After running this test, you should see a summary that tells you which lines of your actual code have been tested by the FxCop unit tests. If your actual code has not been tested, consider using this coverage report to add additional tests.

The `Debug.Assert()` statement in the Pex generated test code is to show the expected outcome of the Remove() method. Using condition `Old_arrListCount != Old_arrListLastIndexOfX`, we are checking whether the ArrayList count and last index of an element are not the same - implying that Remove() method has correctly reduced the count.

To use code coverage for your actual test project, see the following link: [https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd264912.aspx](https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/dd264912.aspx)

Remember to run the tests in a non-debug build mode, to ensure you get reliable test results.

The actual execution of this Pex test with a coverage report will provide the desired results. Please note that Pex may not cover all possible execution paths of a unit test method and it is always necessary to write additional unit tests for edge cases and other potential scenarios.

## <a name="concepts"></a>Important Concepts

- **Property-based testing**: This is a form of automated testing where an application’s state is not specified, but a property it must satisfy is. In other words, properties define what is expected of your application and unit tests are designed to check these properties.
- **Code Contracts**: In computer programming, a code contract is a formal statement or condition that the programmer writes down to help ensure the program maintains the desired properties.
- **Postcondition**: A statement specifying a condition that must hold after the execution of a method/constructor.
- **Precondition**: A statement specifying a condition that must hold prior to the execution of a method/constructor.
- **Invariant**: A property that has to always hold true in the execution of a certain set of operations.
- **Assert**: It's a way to express assumptions about the state of the program.
- **Exceptions**: Exceptions are event notifications that allow an application to respond to errors or abnormal conditions by "catching" the error and, typically, providing control to an error-handling function.

